# NoSQL and Modern Databases: Beyond the Relational Model

> For forty years the relational database was the only answer to
> "where should we store this?" — and then the web happened. Billions
> of users, global replication, unstructured JSON documents, and
> graphs of friendships pushed the classic model to its limits. NoSQL
> databases answered by trading away some guarantees to buy scale and
> flexibility, and modern systems now blend both worlds.

## 1. Why Relational Databases Strain

The relational model — tables, rows, SQL joins, ACID transactions —
remains superb for structured data with rich query patterns: banking
ledgers, inventory, payroll. The strain appears when applications
need:

| Pressure | Why SQL strains | What breaks |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Massive write volume | Single-node ACID coordination | Vertical scaling hits a ceiling |
| Flexible schemas | ALTER TABLE on huge tables | Every column must fit every row |
| Global replication | Synchronous cross-region commits | Latency balloons |
| Deep relationships | Many-way JOINs | Queries slow as graphs grow |

NoSQL (coined for a 2009 meetup, "Not Only SQL") is a family of
storage engines that relax one or more relational assumptions to
serve these patterns.

## 2. The Four Families

[definition]
**NoSQL databases** store and retrieve data without a fixed
table-join-centric model. The four main families are key-value,
document, column-family (wide-column), and graph — each shaped by the
access pattern it optimises.
[/definition]

@diagram tree id="nosql-families-tree" layout="tree" aria="Classification of NoSQL database families"
  "NoSQL" -> "Key-Value"
  "NoSQL" -> "Document"
  "NoSQL" -> "Column-Family"
  "NoSQL" -> "Graph"
  "Key-Value" -> "Redis, DynamoDB"
  "Document" -> "MongoDB, CouchDB"
  "Column-Family" -> "Cassandra, HBase"
  "Graph" -> "Neo4j, Neptune"
@/diagram

**Key-value stores** treat the database as a giant hash map: `GET`
and `PUT` by key, no queries inside values. They are breathtakingly
fast and simple — the natural home of sessions, caches, and shopping
carts. Redis even holds everything in memory.

**Document stores** keep self-contained JSON-like documents
(`users`, `orders`, `posts`) that may differ field by field. A
document is fetched whole — no joins — which mirrors how web pages
use data. MongoDB queries documents with JSON-shaped filters:

```javascript
db.users.find({ city: "Pune", age: { $gte: 18 } })
```

**Column-family stores** split rows into column families that live
and are read together, spreading them across many machines. Cassandra
tunes for relentless write throughput (time-series, IoT, messaging).

**Graph databases** make relationships first-class citizens: nodes
and edges you can walk in constant hops. "Which friends of Ravi
bought something his sister reviewed?" is one graph traversal — and
a recursive-join nightmare in SQL.

## 3. CAP Theorem: The Impossibility Triangle

[important]
A distributed data store can strongly provide at most **two** of:
**Consistency** (every read sees the latest write), **Availability**
(every request gets a response), and **Partition tolerance (P)**
(the system survives network splits). Since partitions *will* happen
in any real network, the practical choice is between C and A **when
a partition strikes** — CP systems (many SQL clusters, MongoDB
defaults, HBase) may refuse requests; AP systems (Cassandra, CouchDB,
DynamoDB) answer possibly-stale data.
[/important]

**Eventual consistency** is the AP compromise: replicas drift apart
during a partition and converge once the network heals. Shopping
carts and social feeds tolerate seconds of staleness; bank balances
and inventory decrements usually do not — which is why serious
systems use the right store per use case, not one store for
everything.

## 4. ACID vs BASE

| ACID (relational) | BASE (many NoSQL) |
| --- | --- |
| Atomicity — all or nothing | Basically Available |
| Consistency — constraints always hold | Soft state |
| Isolation — transactions don't interleave | Eventual consistency |
| Durability — committed writes survive crashes | — |

Neither is "better": ACID protects correctness under contention;
BASE buys availability and scale with delayed convergence. Payment
routing wants ACID; a "like" counter wants BASE.

## 5. When to Choose What

- **Choose SQL (PostgreSQL, MySQL)** when data is relational, queries
  are ad hoc and join-heavy, and transactional integrity matters —
  still the default answer for most applications.
- **Choose key-value (Redis, DynamoDB)** for caching, sessions,
  leaderboards — small values, hot keys, microsecond reads.
- **Choose document (MongoDB)** for flexible, evolving JSON-shaped
  records: catalogues, content management, user profiles.
- **Choose column-family (Cassandra)** for append-heavy,
  partition-friendly workloads at enormous scale: logs, telemetry,
  chat history.
- **Choose graph (Neo4j)** when the *relationship* is the question:
  recommendations, fraud rings, knowledge graphs, network topology.

[example]
An e-commerce platform commonly uses five stores at once: PostgreSQL
for orders and payments (ACID), Redis for carts and sessions
(speed), MongoDB for the product catalogue (flexible attributes),
Cassandra for click-stream analytics (write volume), and Neo4j for
recommendations (graph walks). Polyglot persistence — choosing
several purpose-fit stores — is the modern default, not an
exotic exception.
[/example]

## 6. NewSQL and the Blended Future

A newer wave — **NewSQL** (CockroachDB, Google Spanner, TiDB) —
delivers SQL's ACID guarantees with NoSQL's horizontal scale, using
distributed consensus (Raft/Paxos) and clever clock synchronisation.
Meanwhile PostgreSQL gained JSONB documents and full-text search,
blurring the boundary from the other side. Vector databases
(Pinecone, pgvector) extend storage toward machine-learning
embeddings — similarity search is becoming a first-class query type.
The industry's direction is clear: the question is no longer
"SQL or NoSQL?" but "which guarantees does each workload actually
need?"

[summary]
NoSQL is a family of stores trading relational guarantees for scale
and flexibility: key-value (Redis) for blinding lookups, document
(MongoDB) for flexible JSON records, column-family (Cassandra) for
relentless writes, and graph (Neo4j) for relationship-first queries.
The CAP theorem forces a consistency-versus-availability choice
during network partitions, giving CP and AP flavours and the
eventual-consistency compromise; BASE is the relaxed counterpart of
ACID. Pragmatic systems practise polyglot persistence — SQL for
money, key-value for speed, documents for flexibility — while
NewSQL (Spanner, CockroachDB) and JSONB-enabled PostgreSQL blend the
best of both worlds. Choose per workload, not per fashion.
[/summary]
